## 1. System Architecture & Linux Permissions Blueprint

### 1.1 The two system users

```bash
# ── Site workload user — owns everything under /home/beacon. NO sudo. ──
useradd --create-home --home-dir /home/beacon --shell /bin/bash --user-group beacon
usermod --append --groups www-data beacon          # can create www-data-group files
chown beacon:www-data /home/beacon
chmod 750 /home/beacon                              # nginx traverses via group; not world-readable

# ── Panel user — owns nothing under /home/beacon. Holds all sudo. ──
useradd --system --create-home --home-dir /home/beacon-panel \
        --shell /usr/sbin/nologin --user-group beacon-panel

install -d -o beacon -g beacon -m 0700 /home/beacon/.ssh
install -d -o beacon -g beacon -m 0700 /home/beacon/.beacon
install -d -o beacon -g beacon -m 0750 /home/beacon/.beacon/{bin,deploy}

# Panel-written logs live outside /home/beacon so the panel needs no access there
install -d -o beacon-panel -g beacon-panel -m 0750 /var/log/beacon/{deployments,commands}
install -d -o beacon -g beacon-panel -m 0750 /var/log/beacon/sites      # supervisor stdout

install -d -o www-data -g www-data -m 0755 /var/www/beacon-acme         # shared ACME webroot
```

`beacon` keeps a real login shell (`/bin/bash`) — required for `crontab`, Supervisor `environment`, and `bash -lc` deploy scripts. `beacon-panel` gets `nologin`; it is never logged into, only `sudo`-targeted.

### 1.2 Filesystem layout

```
/home/beacon/                              750 beacon:www-data   ← site tier, NO sudo
├── .ssh/id_ed25519_<site>                 600 beacon:beacon
├── .beacon/
│   ├── bin/<site>-server.sh               750  — SSR launcher (Supervisor exec target)
│   └── deploy/<site>.sh                   700  — user-editable deployment script
└── example.com/                          2750 beacon:www-data
    ├── .env                               640 beacon:www-data
    ├── storage/tmp/                       700 beacon:beacon    — per-site sys_temp_dir
    ├── storage/sessions/                  700 beacon:beacon    — per-site session.save_path
    └── public/ | dist/ | .next/ | .output/

/opt/beacon/                                                     ← panel tier
├── bin/beacon-{nginx,php,supervisor,certbot,service,pkg,cron,fs,run,update}
│                                          755 root:root         — beacon-panel executes, cannot write
└── panel/
    ├── releases/2026-08-03-120000/        755 root:beacon-panel — immutable after promotion
    ├── current -> releases/…              symlink, swapped atomically by root
    └── shared/
        ├── .env                           640 root:beacon-panel — panel READS, cannot write
        ├── beacon.sqlite                  640 beacon-panel:beacon-panel
        ├── storage/                      2770 beacon-panel:beacon-panel
        └── bootstrap-cache/              2770 beacon-panel:beacon-panel

/var/log/beacon/{deployments,commands}/     750 beacon-panel     — panel owns the Process pipe
/var/log/beacon/sites/                      750 beacon:beacon-panel
/etc/sudoers.d/beacon-panel                 440 root:root
```

> **Why deployment logs live in `/var/log/beacon` and not `/home/beacon`:** the panel captures the child process's stdout through the Symfony `Process` pipe and **writes the log file itself**. The log is therefore authored by `beacon-panel`, so no cross-user file access is needed for the 1 s polling loop — no `sudo` fork per poll.

### 1.3 The setgid rule (why site dirs are `2750`)

Site directories carry the **setgid bit** so every file `git clone`, `composer`, `npm` or the app creates inherits group `www-data`. Without it, new files land `beacon:beacon` and Nginx starts throwing 403s after the first deploy.

```bash
install -d -o beacon -g www-data -m 2750 /home/beacon/example.com
```

`beacon`'s umask is **0002** (set in `~/.bashrc` and in every launcher), so files land `0664` and directories `2775`. A post-deploy step re-asserts this defensively — all as `beacon`, no sudo:

```bash
find "$BEACON_SITE_DIR" -type d -exec chmod 2775 {} +
find "$BEACON_SITE_DIR" -type f -exec chmod 0664 {} +
chmod -R ug+rwX "$BEACON_SITE_DIR"/{storage,bootstrap/cache} 2>/dev/null || true
chmod 0640 "$BEACON_SITE_DIR/.env"
chmod 0700 "$BEACON_SITE_DIR"/storage/{tmp,sessions} 2>/dev/null || true
```

*(POSIX ACLs were considered as an alternative to the `beacon-fs` wrapper for panel→site writes and rejected: `chmod -R` above clears the ACL mask, which would make permissions silently drift.)*

### 1.4 PHP-FPM pool: one pool per site

`/etc/php/8.3/fpm/pool.d/example-com.conf`:

```ini
; Managed by Beacon — regenerated on save. Manual edits will be overwritten.
[example-com]
user  = beacon
group = www-data

listen       = /run/php/php8.3-fpm-example-com.sock
listen.owner = beacon-panel      ; only the panel needs to stat it
listen.group = www-data          ; nginx connects via group
listen.mode  = 0660

pm                       = ondemand      ; idle sites cost zero RSS
pm.max_children          = 20
pm.process_idle_timeout  = 10s
pm.max_requests          = 500

chdir = {{ $site->path }}

catch_workers_output    = yes
decorate_workers_output = no

php_admin_value[error_log]     = /var/log/beacon/sites/{{ $site->name }}-php-error.log
php_admin_flag[log_errors]     = on
php_admin_flag[display_errors] = off

php_admin_value[memory_limit]        = {{ $ini['memory_limit'] }}
php_admin_value[upload_max_filesize] = {{ $ini['upload_max_filesize'] }}
php_admin_value[post_max_size]       = {{ $ini['post_max_size'] }}
php_admin_value[max_execution_time]  = {{ $ini['max_execution_time'] }}

; ── Tenant hardening (audit §0.2) ──────────────────────────────────
; Per-site temp + session dirs. Removes the shared /tmp and the shared
; session.save_path cross-tenant channels, and means /tmp is NOT needed
; in open_basedir at all.
php_admin_value[sys_temp_dir]      = {{ $site->path }}/storage/tmp
php_admin_value[upload_tmp_dir]    = {{ $site->path }}/storage/tmp
php_admin_value[session.save_path] = {{ $site->path }}/storage/sessions
@if ($site->open_basedir)
; FPM SAPI only — CLI (composer, artisan, npm) is unaffected.
; Hardening, NOT a boundary: exec() bypasses open_basedir entirely.
php_admin_value[open_basedir] = {{ $site->path }}:/usr/share/php{{ $extraPaths }}
@endif
@if ($site->strict_functions)
php_admin_value[disable_functions] = exec,passthru,shell_exec,system,proc_open,popen
@endif
```

The socket is `beacon-panel:www-data 0660`: Nginx connects via the group, the site user cannot even `stat` another site's socket. Worker processes are `beacon`, so PHP writes files the site user owns.

**`php_admin_value` vs `php_value`:** per-site tuning uses `php_admin_value` so `ini_set()` cannot escape it. Per-*version* defaults (the "php.ini Quick Config" screen) go to `/etc/php/<X.Y>/{fpm,cli}/conf.d/99-beacon.ini`; a site's pool value always wins.

Pool changes need `systemctl restart php8.3-fpm` (reload does not re-read `pool.d/`). Beacon **debounces** this — toggling five extensions restarts FPM once.

### 1.5 Sudo boundary

**`beacon` has no sudoers entry at all.** Everything below is granted only to `beacon-panel`.

```
# /etc/sudoers.d/beacon-panel    root:root 0440
# Installed only after `visudo -cf` validates it.
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_NGINX  = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-nginx
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_PHP    = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-php
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_SUP    = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-supervisor
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_SSL    = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-certbot
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_SVC    = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-service
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_PKG    = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-pkg
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_CRON   = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-cron
Cmnd_Alias BEACON_UPDATE = /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-update

Defaults!BEACON_NGINX,BEACON_PHP,BEACON_SUP,BEACON_SSL,BEACON_SVC,BEACON_PKG,BEACON_CRON,BEACON_UPDATE \
    !requiretty, env_reset, secure_path="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"

# Root-privileged system mutation
beacon-panel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: BEACON_NGINX, BEACON_PHP, BEACON_SUP, BEACON_SSL, \
                                  BEACON_SVC, BEACON_PKG, BEACON_CRON, BEACON_UPDATE

# Unprivileged runas — everything that must execute *as a site*.
# Note the runas target is (beacon), NOT (root), and both take zero arguments:
# the entire job spec arrives on stdin, so there is no argv attack surface.
beacon-panel ALL=(beacon) NOPASSWD: /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-run, /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-fs
```

| Needs root | Needs `sudo -u beacon` | Needs neither |
|---|---|---|
| `/etc/nginx/**`, `nginx -t`, reload | Deploy scripts, Console commands | Panel's own files, DB, logs |
| `pool.d/**`, `phpenmod`, FPM restart | `git`, `composer`, `npm`, `bun`, `artisan` | `systemctl is-active` / `show` |
| `/etc/supervisor/conf.d/**`, `supervisorctl` | Read/write site `.env` + deploy script | `/proc/stat`, `/proc/meminfo`, `statvfs` |
| `certbot certonly` / `delete` | | MySQL DDL (`beacon_admin` SQL account) |
| `systemctl restart` on a **whitelisted unit list** | | |
| `crontab -u beacon`, `apt-get install php*` | | |

**Rules that make this safe:**

1. **No wildcards in sudoers.** Ten fixed absolute paths, nothing else.
2. Wrappers are `root:root 0755` — `beacon-panel` executes but never modifies them.
3. Every argument matched against a **strict allow-list regex**; anything else exits `64`.
4. **File content travels on stdin, never argv** — no arg-injection, no `ARG_MAX` limit, no secrets in `ps`.
5. Wrappers `set -euo pipefail`, quote every expansion, never `eval`.
6. **Reserved-name guard:** the panel's own vhost, pool and Supervisor programs (`beacon-panel*`) are refused, so the UI cannot delete the panel out from under itself.
7. **Content validation, not just argument validation** (new in Rev 2): the Supervisor wrapper rejects any config containing `user\s*=\s*root` or a `command` outside `/home/beacon`, `/usr/bin`, `/usr/local`. Without this, a compromised panel could escalate to root through a Supervisor unit — see §1.7.

### 1.6 Reference wrapper: `/opt/beacon/bin/beacon-nginx`

The pattern every root wrapper follows — validate, stage, test, roll back.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-nginx — root:root 0755
# Exit codes: 0 ok | 64 bad usage/argument | 65 nginx config rejected | 66 not found
set -euo pipefail
umask 022

SITES_AVAILABLE=/etc/nginx/sites-available
SITES_ENABLED=/etc/nginx/sites-enabled
NGINX=/usr/sbin/nginx
SYSTEMCTL=/usr/bin/systemctl
RESERVED='^beacon-panel$'

die() { printf 'beacon-nginx: %s\n' "$*" >&2; exit 64; }

valid_name() {
    local n="${1:-}"
    [[ -n "$n" ]]                                        || die "missing site name"
    [[ "$n" =~ ^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9.-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?$ ]]  || die "invalid site name: $n"
    [[ "$n" != *".."* ]]                                 || die "invalid site name: $n"
}
guard_reserved() { [[ ! "$1" =~ $RESERVED ]] || die "refusing to modify reserved vhost: $1"; }

action="${1:-}"; shift || true

case "$action" in
    write)
        name="${1:-}"; valid_name "$name"; guard_reserved "$name"
        target="$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name"
        # mktemp on the SAME filesystem so the final mv is atomic
        tmp="$(mktemp "$SITES_AVAILABLE/.beacon-XXXXXX")"
        trap 'rm -f "$tmp"' EXIT
        cat > "$tmp"                          # <-- config arrives on STDIN
        [[ -s "$tmp" ]] || die "empty configuration"
        chmod 0644 "$tmp"

        backup=""
        if [[ -f "$target" ]]; then backup="$(mktemp)"; cp -a "$target" "$backup"; fi
        mv -f "$tmp" "$target"; trap - EXIT

        if ! out="$("$NGINX" -t 2>&1)"; then
            if [[ -n "$backup" ]]; then mv -f "$backup" "$target"; else rm -f "$target"; fi
            printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2
            exit 65                            # panel surfaces this verbatim in the UI
        fi
        [[ -n "$backup" ]] && rm -f "$backup"
        printf '%s\n' "$out" ;;

    enable)
        name="${1:-}"; valid_name "$name"
        [[ -f "$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name" ]] || exit 66
        ln -sfn "$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name" "$SITES_ENABLED/$name"
        if ! out="$("$NGINX" -t 2>&1)"; then
            rm -f "$SITES_ENABLED/$name"; printf '%s\n' "$out" >&2; exit 65
        fi ;;

    disable) name="${1:-}"; valid_name "$name"; guard_reserved "$name"; rm -f "$SITES_ENABLED/$name" ;;
    delete)  name="${1:-}"; valid_name "$name"; guard_reserved "$name"
             rm -f "$SITES_ENABLED/$name" "$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name" ;;
    read)    name="${1:-}"; valid_name "$name"
             [[ -f "$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name" ]] || exit 66; cat "$SITES_AVAILABLE/$name" ;;
    test)    "$NGINX" -t 2>&1 || exit 65 ;;
    reload)  "$NGINX" -t 2>&1 || exit 65; "$SYSTEMCTL" reload nginx ;;
    *)       die "unknown action: ${action:-<none>}" ;;
esac
```

> **Why write-then-test-then-rollback:** `nginx -t` validates the *entire* configuration tree, so a candidate vhost can only be meaningfully tested once it is on disk. Staging it, testing, and restoring the backup on failure is the only way to validate without a window where a bad config could survive a reload. This satisfies the spec's critical safety rule — **an invalid vhost never reaches a running Nginx**.

The other root wrappers follow identically:

- **`beacon-php`** — `pool-write <site> <ver>` (stdin), `pool-delete`, `ini-write <ver> <sapi>` (stdin), `ext-enable|ext-disable <ver> <ext>`, `fpm-restart <ver>`. Version `^8\.[1-4]$`, extension `^[a-z0-9_]{1,32}$`.
- **`beacon-supervisor`** — `write <program>` (stdin, **content-validated**, §1.7), `delete`, `reread-update`, `start|stop|restart <program>`, `status`.
- **`beacon-certbot`** — `issue <email> <domain…>` via `certonly --webroot -w /var/www/beacon-acme`, `renew`, `delete <lineage>`, `certificates`. Per-label domain regex, 100-domain cap.
- **`beacon-service`** — `status|restart|start|stop <unit>`, `<unit>` ∈ `^(nginx|mysql|redis-server|supervisor|php8\.[1-4]-fpm)$`.
- **`beacon-pkg`** — `php-install|php-remove <ver>`, `ext-install <ver> <ext>` against an allow-list.
- **`beacon-cron`** — `write` (stdin) → `crontab -u beacon -`; `read` → `crontab -u beacon -l`.
- **`beacon-update`** — panel self-update, §1.8.

### 1.7 Content validation in `beacon-supervisor` (new in Rev 2)

Argument validation is not enough for wrappers that accept a config *body*. A Supervisor unit can specify `user=root`, which would turn a panel compromise into root execution.

```bash
# /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-supervisor  (write action, excerpt)
write)
    prog="${1:-}"
    [[ "$prog" =~ ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,63}$ ]] || die "invalid program name"
    [[ ! "$prog" =~ ^beacon-panel ]]             || die "reserved program name"

    tmp="$(mktemp /etc/supervisor/conf.d/.beacon-XXXXXX)"
    trap 'rm -f "$tmp"' EXIT
    cat > "$tmp"

    # ── Content policy: a Supervisor unit must never escalate ──
    grep -Eqi '^[[:space:]]*user[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*root' "$tmp" \
        && die "supervisor unit may not run as root"
    grep -Eq  '^[[:space:]]*user[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*beacon[[:space:]]*$' "$tmp" \
        || die "supervisor unit must declare user = beacon"
    grep -Eq  '^[[:space:]]*command[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*(/home/beacon/|/usr/bin/|/usr/local/)' "$tmp" \
        || die "command must live under /home/beacon, /usr/bin or /usr/local"

    chmod 0644 "$tmp"
    mv -f "$tmp" "/etc/supervisor/conf.d/${prog}.conf"; trap - EXIT
    /usr/bin/supervisorctl reread >/dev/null && /usr/bin/supervisorctl update
    ;;
```

### 1.8 Panel self-update — `beacon-update` (audit §0.1)

The `releases`/`current` pattern: **build unprivileged, promote as root, health-gate, auto-rollback.** Composer and npm never run as root, so a malicious package cannot execute privileged code — and the promoted tree is root-owned, so a later panel RCE cannot plant a persistent backdoor in it.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-update — root:root 0755
set -euo pipefail
ROOT=/opt/beacon/panel
REPO=https://github.com/<org>/beacon.git
KEEP=3

case "${1:-deploy}" in
deploy)
    ref="${2:-}"; [[ "$ref" =~ ^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]] || { echo "bad tag" >&2; exit 64; }
    rel="$ROOT/releases/$(date -u +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)"

    # ── 1. Build as beacon-panel. Never as root. ──
    install -d -o beacon-panel -g beacon-panel -m 0755 "$rel"
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- git clone --depth 1 --branch "$ref" "$REPO" "$rel"
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- git -C "$rel" verify-tag "$ref" || echo "WARN: unsigned tag $ref" >&2
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- composer install -d "$rel" --no-dev --no-interaction \
                                        --prefer-dist --optimize-autoloader
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- npm --prefix "$rel" ci
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- npm --prefix "$rel" run build

    # ── 2. Wire shared state ──
    rm -rf "$rel/storage" "$rel/bootstrap/cache"
    ln -sfn "$ROOT/shared/.env"            "$rel/.env"
    ln -sfn "$ROOT/shared/storage"         "$rel/storage"
    ln -sfn "$ROOT/shared/bootstrap-cache" "$rel/bootstrap/cache"

    runuser -u beacon-panel -- php "$rel/artisan" migrate --force
    runuser -u beacon-panel -- php "$rel/artisan" optimize

    # ── 3. Freeze the tree, then promote atomically ──
    chown -R root:beacon-panel "$rel"; chmod -R go-w "$rel"
    prev="$(readlink -f "$ROOT/current" || true)"
    ln -sfn "$rel" "$ROOT/current.new" && mv -Tf "$ROOT/current.new" "$ROOT/current"

    systemctl restart php8.3-fpm            # resets OPcache for the new realpath
    supervisorctl restart beacon-panel-worker

    # ── 4. Health-gate on the starter kit's existing /up endpoint ──
    for i in $(seq 1 15); do
        sleep 1
        curl -fsS --max-time 3 http://127.0.0.1/up >/dev/null 2>&1 && ok=1 && break
    done
    if [[ "${ok:-0}" != 1 && -n "$prev" ]]; then
        echo "health check failed — rolling back to $prev" >&2
        ln -sfn "$prev" "$ROOT/current.new" && mv -Tf "$ROOT/current.new" "$ROOT/current"
        systemctl restart php8.3-fpm; supervisorctl restart beacon-panel-worker
        exit 70
    fi
    ls -1dt "$ROOT"/releases/*/ | tail -n +$((KEEP+1)) | xargs -r rm -rf ;;

rollback)
    target="$(ls -1dt "$ROOT"/releases/*/ | sed -n 2p)"
    [[ -n "$target" ]] || { echo "no previous release" >&2; exit 66; }
    ln -sfn "$target" "$ROOT/current.new" && mv -Tf "$ROOT/current.new" "$ROOT/current"
    systemctl restart php8.3-fpm; supervisorctl restart beacon-panel-worker ;;
esac
```

The panel vhost's root is `/opt/beacon/panel/current/public`. The Laravel template's `fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $realpath_root$fastcgi_script_name` already resolves the symlink to the real release path — which is exactly why `$realpath_root` (not `$document_root`) is used, and why OPcache does not serve a stale tree after promotion.

**`.env` is `root:beacon-panel 0640` — the panel reads it and cannot write it.** This is achievable because all mutable configuration (GitHub App key, server settings, MySQL credentials for *site* databases) lives encrypted in SQLite, not in `.env`. Only `install.sh` and `beacon-update` write it, both as root.

### 1.9 `beacon-run` — executing work as a site (new in Rev 2)

This replaces "the panel is already `beacon`". It takes **zero arguments**; the whole job spec arrives as JSON on stdin, so there is no argv surface and no `sudo` env-plumbing.

```python
#!/usr/bin/python3
# /opt/beacon/bin/beacon-run — root:root 0755, invoked as: sudo -u beacon beacon-run
# stdin: {"cwd": "...", "argv": [...], "env": {...}}
import json, os, sys

ALLOWED_ROOTS = ("/home/beacon/",)
ENV_ALLOW_PREFIX = ("BEACON_", "NODE_", "NPM_", "npm_", "COMPOSER_", "GIT_", "CI",
                    "PATH", "HOME", "USER", "LANG", "LC_ALL", "PORT", "HOST")

def fail(msg, code=64):
    print(f"beacon-run: {msg}", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(code)

try:
    spec = json.load(sys.stdin)
except ValueError:
    fail("malformed job spec")

cwd = os.path.realpath(spec.get("cwd", ""))
if not any(cwd.startswith(r) for r in ALLOWED_ROOTS) or not os.path.isdir(cwd):
    fail(f"cwd outside permitted roots: {cwd}")

argv = spec.get("argv") or []
if not isinstance(argv, list) or not all(isinstance(a, str) for a in argv) or not argv:
    fail("argv must be a non-empty list of strings")

env = {k: str(v) for k, v in (spec.get("env") or {}).items()
       if isinstance(k, str) and k.startswith(ENV_ALLOW_PREFIX)}
env.setdefault("HOME", "/home/beacon")
env.setdefault("USER", "beacon")
env.setdefault("PATH", "/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin")

os.chdir(cwd)
os.umask(0o002)
# Make builds the OOM killer's preferred victim, never mysqld (audit §0.3)
if spec.get("oom_expendable"):
    try:
        open("/proc/self/oom_score_adj", "w").write("750")
    except OSError:
        pass
os.execvpe(argv[0], argv, env)
```

`/opt/beacon/bin/beacon-fs` is its sibling for file I/O the panel needs inside `/home/beacon` (`read`/`write`/`stat`/`tail` on a canonicalised, prefix-checked path, content on stdin). Both run **as `beacon`, not root**, so a bug in either yields no privilege gain.

