```

### 3.d Supervisor — Laravel queue worker

```ini
{{-- resources/views/supervisor/queue-worker.blade.php --}}
; ─── Managed by Beacon — do not edit by hand ───
[program:{{ $process->program_name }}]
process_name    = %(program_name)s_%(process_num)02d
command         = /usr/bin/php{{ $site->php_version }} {{ $site->path }}/artisan queue:work {{ $process->connection }} --queue={{ $process->queue }} --sleep={{ $process->sleep }} --tries={{ $process->tries }} --max-time={{ $process->max_time }} --timeout={{ $process->job_timeout }}
directory       = {{ $site->path }}
user            = beacon
numprocs        = {{ $process->numprocs }}
autostart       = {{ $process->autostart ? 'true' : 'false' }}
autorestart     = {{ $process->autorestart ? 'true' : 'false' }}
startsecs       = 5
stopasgroup     = true
killasgroup     = true
stopsignal      = {{ $process->stop_signal }}
; Must exceed --timeout so an in-flight job is never killed mid-write
stopwaitsecs    = {{ $process->stop_wait_secs }}
redirect_stderr = true
stdout_logfile  = /var/log/beacon/sites/{{ $site->name }}-{{ $process->name }}.log
stdout_logfile_maxbytes = 10MB
stdout_logfile_backups  = 3
environment     = HOME="/home/beacon",USER="beacon"
```

Form defaults: `connection=redis`, `queue=default`, `sleep=3`, `tries=3`, `job_timeout=90`, `max_time=3600`, `stop_wait_secs=3600`, `numprocs=1`. `--max-time=3600` recycles workers hourly so a leaking app never OOMs the box; `stop_wait_secs > job_timeout` is enforced by the FormRequest. `user = beacon` is literal, not templated — the wrapper's content policy (§1.7) rejects anything else.

### 3.e Supervisor — SSR daemon

Supervisor's `environment=` is one line with awkward `%` escaping, so Beacon generates a **launcher script** instead. It is readable and editable by the developer, and `exec` guarantees signals reach Node rather than an `npm` wrapper.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /home/beacon/.beacon/bin/example.com-server.sh — 0750 beacon:beacon
set -euo pipefail
umask 0002
cd /home/beacon/example.com
set -a; [ -f .env ] && . ./.env; set +a
export PATH="/usr/local/node/v22/bin:$PATH"
export NODE_ENV=production HOST=127.0.0.1 PORT=3001
# exec → PID is preserved, Supervisor's SIGTERM reaches Node directly
exec node_modules/.bin/next start -H "$HOST" -p "$PORT"   # Nuxt: exec node .output/server/index.mjs
```

```ini
{{-- resources/views/supervisor/ssr.blade.php --}}
[program:{{ $process->program_name }}]
command         = {{ $process->command }}
directory       = {{ $site->path }}
user            = beacon
numprocs        = 1
autostart       = true
autorestart     = true
startsecs       = 10
startretries    = 3
stopasgroup     = true
killasgroup     = true
stopsignal      = SIGTERM
stopwaitsecs    = 20
redirect_stderr = true
stdout_logfile  = /var/log/beacon/sites/{{ $site->name }}-ssr.log
stdout_logfile_maxbytes = 10MB
stdout_logfile_backups  = 3
environment     = HOME="/home/beacon",USER="beacon"
```

### 3.f Cron: the managed block

Beacon owns a delimited region of `beacon`'s crontab and rewrites it wholesale from the DB, preserving hand-written entries outside it. Written through `beacon-cron write` (root wrapper → `crontab -u beacon -`).

```cron
# >>> BEACON MANAGED BLOCK — generated {{ $timestamp }}. Do not edit between markers. <<<
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
* * * * * cd /home/beacon/example.com && /usr/bin/php8.3 artisan schedule:run >> /dev/null 2>&1
0 3 * * * cd /home/beacon/example.com && /usr/bin/php8.3 artisan backup:run >> /var/log/beacon/sites/example.com-cron.log 2>&1
# <<< END BEACON MANAGED BLOCK >>>
```

The panel's own scheduler runs in `beacon-panel`'s crontab, not `beacon`'s — a site can no longer see or touch it.

### 3.g systemd OOM protection (audit §0.3)

```ini
# /etc/systemd/system/mysql.service.d/99-beacon-oom.conf   (same for redis-server, nginx, php8.3-fpm)
[Service]
OOMScoreAdjust=-500
```

Paired with `choom`-equivalent `oom_score_adj=750` on build subtrees (§1.9), the kernel's victim ordering becomes deterministic: **the build dies, the database survives.**

